  "historical_output_text": " Watering a Houseplant\n\nStep 1: Assess the soil moisture level. Gently poke your finger into the soil about an inch deep. If it feels dry, it’s time to water.\n\nStep 2: Choose the right watering method. For most plants, a watering can with a narrow spout is ideal as it allows for a steady stream and precise watering.\n\nStep 3: Water thoroughly. Apply water directly to the soil surface, pouring slowly and steadily until the water begins to drain out the bottom of the pot. This ensures the whole root ball is wet, promoting healthy growth.\n\nStep 4: Avoid overwatering. Overwatering can lead to root rot, which is detrimental to your plant. Wait until the soil is dry to the touch before watering again.\n\nStep 5: Drain excess water. If your pot doesn’t have drainage holes, use a saucer and empty it after watering to prevent standing water, which can lead to fungal diseases.\n\nStep 6: Check for signs of distress. After watering, inspect your plant for wilting or yellowing leaves, which could indicate overwatering or other issues.\n\nStep 7: Clean",
